.Dd 2025-09-30
.Dt SSL_GET_SHARED_SIGALGS 3ossl
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm SSL_get_shared_sigalgs ,
.Nm SSL_get_sigalgs
.Nd get supported signature algorithms
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In openssl/ssl.h
.Ft int
.Fo SSL_get_shared_sigalgs
.Fa "SSL *s"
.Fa "int idx"
.Fa "int *psign"
.Fa "int *phash"
.Fa "int *psignhash"
.Fa "unsigned char *rsig"
.Fa "unsigned char *rhash"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo SSL_get_sigalgs
.Fa "SSL *s"
.Fa "int idx"
.Fa "int *psign"
.Fa "int *phash"
.Fa "int *psignhash"
.Fa "unsigned char *rsig"
.Fa "unsigned char *rhash"
.Fc
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn SSL_get_shared_sigalgs
returns information about the shared signature algorithms supported by
peer
.Fa s .
The parameter
.Fa idx
indicates the index of the shared signature algorithm to return starting
from zero.
The signature algorithm NID is written to
.Sy *psign ,
the hash NID to
.Sy *phash
and the sign and hash NID to
.Sy *psignhash .
The raw signature and hash values are written to
.Sy *rsig
and
.Sy *rhash .
.Pp
.Fn SSL_get_sigalgs
is similar to
.Fn SSL_get_shared_sigalgs
except it returns information about all signature algorithms supported
by
.Fa s
in the order they were sent by the peer.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn SSL_get_shared_sigalgs
and
.Fn SSL_get_sigalgs
return the number of signature algorithms or
.Sy 0
if the
.Fa idx
parameter is out of range.
.Sh NOTES
These functions are typically called for debugging purposes (to report
the peer's preferences) or where an application wants finer control over
certificate selection.
Most applications will rely on internal handling and will not need to
call them.
.Pp
If an application is only interested in the highest preference shared
signature algorithm it can just set
.Fa idx
to zero.
.Pp
Any or all of the parameters
.Fa psign ,
.Fa phash ,
.Fa psignhash ,
.Fa rsig
or
.Fa rhash
can be set to
.Dv NULL
if the value is not required.
By setting them all to
.Dv NULL
and setting
.Fa idx
to zero the total number of signature algorithms can be determined:
which can be zero.
.Pp
These functions must be called after the peer has sent a list of
supported signature algorithms: after a client hello (for servers) or a
certificate request (for clients).
They can (for example) be called in the certificate callback.
.Pp
Only TLS 1.2, TLS 1.3 and DTLS 1.2 currently support signature
algorithms.
If these functions are called on an earlier version of TLS or DTLS zero
is returned.
.Pp
The shared signature algorithms returned by
.Fn SSL_get_shared_sigalgs
are ordered according to configuration and peer preferences.
.Pp
The raw values correspond to the on the wire form as defined by RFC5246
et al.
The NIDs are OpenSSL equivalents.
For example if the peer sent sha256(4) and rsa(1) then
.Sy *rhash
would be 4,
.Sy *rsign
1,
.Sy *phash
NID_sha256,
.Sy *psig
NID_rsaEncryption and
.Sy *psignhash
NID_sha256WithRSAEncryption.
.Pp
If a signature algorithm is not recognised the corresponding NIDs will
be set to
.Sy NID_undef .
This may be because the value is not supported, is not an appropriate
combination (for example MD5 and DSA) or the signature algorithm does
not use a hash (for example Ed25519).
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr SSL_CTX_set_cert_cb 3 ,
.Xr ssl 7
